<Base
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description="Cookie policy for DaoismHub — what cookies and similar technologies the sites do and do not use, why no consent banner is shown, hosting security measures, and how to manage cookies in your browser."
>
  <h1>Cookie Policy</h1>
  <p class="lede">Version 1.0 &middot; Last updated: October 23, 2026 &middot; Applies to daoismhub.com and data.daoismhub.com</p>

  <h2>1. What this policy covers</h2>
  <p>
    This page explains how DaoismHub uses cookies and similar technologies — small files or
    identifiers stored in your browser — across <strong>daoismhub.com</strong> (the main
    site) and <strong>data.daoismhub.com</strong> (the Data Registry). It is written in plain
    language, states plainly what we do and do not place in your browser, and tells you how
    to manage cookies if you wish to. It should be read alongside the
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>, which explains what personal data the sites
    process more broadly.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>We set no cookies.</strong> The sites place no first-party cookies, no third-party
    cookies, no tracking pixels, no local storage, no session storage and no similar
    client-side identifiers on your device. Because nothing is placed in your browser, no
    cookie-consent banner is shown and no consent is required to read the sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    The only browser-side activity that touches the sites is the Data Registry's search
    function, which runs entirely in your browser against a static index file and never
    transmits your query to us. Details are in section 8.
  </p>

  <h2>3. What a cookie is</h2>
  <p>
    A cookie is a small text file that a website asks your browser to store, usually so the
    site can recognise you on a later visit. Cookies are commonly used for things like
    keeping you signed in, remembering a preference, measuring traffic, or showing you
    advertising. Similar technologies include local storage and session storage, which store
    data in the browser rather than as a separate file, and tracking pixels or web beacons,
    which are small images used to record when a page is viewed. This policy covers all of
    these, whether or not the word "cookie" is used.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Cookies we set</h2>
  <p>None. Specifically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No first-party cookies.</strong> The sites do not set any cookie on their own domains.</li>
    <li><strong>No local storage or session storage.</strong> The pages do not write to your browser's storage.</li>
    <li><strong>No preference or functionality cookies.</strong> There are no accounts, no dark-mode toggle, no saved settings and no "remember me" to require one.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Third-party cookies</h2>
  <p>
    The sites use no third-party services that would place cookies. There is no analytics
    provider, no advertising network, no tag manager, no social-media plugin, no embedded
    video player, no comment system, no newsletter platform and no CRM connected to the
    pages. The pages embed no third-party widgets or trackers, so no third party is in a
    position to set a cookie through our sites.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Cookie categories, one by one</h2>
  <p>
    Cookie policies commonly divide cookies into categories. Here is how each category
    applies to these sites:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Category</th><th>Typical purpose</th><th>Used by DaoismHub?</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Strictly necessary</td><td>Core function — login, security, cart, page delivery</td><td>No (nothing to log in to; see hosting note in section 7)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Preferences</td><td>Remembering choices such as language or layout</td><td>No (no saved preferences)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Statistics / performance</td><td>Measuring traffic and behaviour</td><td>No (no analytics)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Marketing / targeting</td><td>Advertising and cross-site profiling</td><td>No (no advertising, no tracking)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    In short, none of the four standard cookie categories is used by these sites. The absence
    is deliberate: the sites carry no advertising, no accounts and no sponsored content, so
    they have no reason to track readers. This is the same position stated in the
    <a href="/about/">About</a> page's independence and funding section.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Hosting security measures</h2>
  <p>
    The sites are static and are served through Cloudflare's content-delivery and hosting
    infrastructure. Cloudflare may apply strictly necessary security measures — for example,
    a challenge cookie used to distinguish human visitors from automated attacks. Where such
    measures exist they are set by the infrastructure provider for security purposes, not by
    us, and are not used by us to track you. The
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> (section 5) describes Cloudflare's role as a
    processor in more detail.
  </p>

  <h2>8. The Data Registry search</h2>
  <p>
    The Data Registry's search function runs entirely in your browser: your query is matched
    against a static index file that is downloaded to your device, and it is never
    transmitted to us. There is no search history stored on our side, and no cookie is needed
    to make search work. You can search the Data Registry without an account and without any
    identifier being placed in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Cookies on sites we link to</h2>
  <p>
    Pages link to external resources — for example <strong>ctext.org</strong> for classical
    source texts, or a scholarly publisher for a cited study. Those sites are operated by
    third parties and have their own cookie and privacy policies, which we do not control.
    Following such a link takes you to a site whose cookies are its responsibility, not ours.
    The <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> and <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>
    describe how we treat external links; here, the relevant point is simply that any cookie
    you encounter after leaving our sites was set by a site other than DaoismHub.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Consent and management</h2>
  <p>
    Because we set no cookies and collect nothing through the browser, there is nothing for
    you to consent to and no banner is shown. If you nevertheless want to review or clear
    cookies that other sites have placed in your browser, you can do so at any time through
    your browser's settings:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>View and delete cookies</strong> — every major browser lets you list stored cookies and delete them individually or in bulk.</li>
    <li><strong>Block third-party cookies</strong> — most browsers can block third-party cookies by default.</li>
    <li><strong>Block all cookies</strong> — you can refuse all cookies, including ours (there are none to refuse), without affecting your ability to read the sites, because the sites do not depend on cookies.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Instructions for these settings are provided by your browser vendor, not by us. Deleting
    or blocking cookies will not change how these sites behave for you, because the sites do
    not use them.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control</h2>
  <p>
    We do not track visitors across sites, so there is no cross-site behaviour for a Do Not
    Track or Global Privacy Control signal to stop. We honour such signals in the sense that
    matters: no tracking is performed with or without them. See the
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> (section 11).
  </p>

  <h2>12. Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If we ever introduce a feature that changes this position — analytics, embedded media, a
    contact form, or any cookie-based feature — this policy will be updated <em>before</em>
    that feature goes live, the version number and date at the top will change, the change
    will be described, and, where the law requires, a consent notice will be shown. The
    version published at this address is always the current one.
  </p>

  <h2>13. Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Do I need to accept cookies to read the site?</h3>
  <p>
    No. There are no cookies to accept, and no banner to click through. The pages load
    without placing anything in your browser, so you can read everything with cookies
    blocked or deleted and nothing will change.
  </p>
  <h3>Why don't you use analytics?</h3>
  <p>
    Because the sites are an independent editorial project with no advertising and no
    commercial goal that would be served by measuring readers. We would rather not know
    anything about you than know it and have to protect it. This is the same reason the
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> describes the sites as collecting as close to
    nothing as a website can.
  </p>
  <h3>Why did I see a security challenge on the site?</h3>
  <p>
    Our hosting provider may present a challenge to requests it cannot confidently identify
    as human, in order to protect the sites from automated attack. Any cookie associated
    with that challenge belongs to the infrastructure provider and is a security measure,
    not a tracking mechanism run by us. It is described in section 7.
  </p>
  <h3>Will you add cookies in the future?</h3>
  <p>
    If a future feature requires a cookie — for example a contact form or an embedded media
    player — we will update this policy before the feature goes live and, where the law
    requires, show a consent notice. We have no current plans to introduce any such feature.
  </p>
  <h3>How do I report something that looks like tracking?</h3>
  <p>
    If you believe the sites are placing a cookie or otherwise tracking you, tell us at
    <strong>admin@daoismhub.com</strong> with "Privacy" in the subject line and as
    much detail as you can (the page, the browser, and what you observed). We take such
    reports seriously and will investigate.
  </p>

